WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.260
 I know, it's crazy,
I'm wearing shirts now.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.640
 Hey Marvel fans
Ryan Penagos, a.k.a.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.110
Agent M, here with the VFX
supervisor of Marvel Studios'

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.920
Avengers Endgame Dan DeLeeuw.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:19.720
How you doing, Dan?

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.520
 Good, how are you?

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.340
 Very well.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.740
I would love to
talk about the Hulk.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.260
One of my favorite
reveals in the movie

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.500
is when we see the
new Smart Hulk.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.880
He's a very classy, he's
got great fashion sense,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.080
wonderful glasses.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.040
Where did you and the team
start to look at developing

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.900
this new look for Hulk?

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.600
 Well, we started it with
a couple different ways.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.030
We had, you know,
kind of, the look dev.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.250
They started, kind
of, experimenting

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.330
with different ideas--

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.980
how much Ruffalo, how much Hulk.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.660
And so it was making
sure that we could

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.180
have just enough of Ruffalo
there that, you know, you

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.240
believe him as a character and
help us to bring that character

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.670
to life by what we could do with
bringing the animation into it

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.020
and motion capture into it.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.960
 Mark doing the motion
capture and it seems

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.850
like he's just having a ball.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.110
He's, like, ad-libbing and
even-- like, he's dabbing.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:06.910
 Yeah.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.030
 What is that like for you as
someone who's creating all this

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.870
and being able to express
this amazing CGI character

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.520
with someone like Mark?

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.050
 It's that
partnership, I think,

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:20.200
is key when you get someone of
the caliber of Mark Ruffalo.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.330
He could bring so much because
he knows the character's

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.860
journey through the films, you
know how brilliant of an actor

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.760
he is, and then suddenly
he's bringing that to you.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.330
And then for the visual
effects side of it

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.750
you want to be in a place
that you have the artistry

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.760
and the technology
at a place that

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.210
can actually incorporate that
into the final visual effect.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.520
His face is so elastic
and his big cheeks--

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.980
it's like you need to be able
to get all of that and, kind of,

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.840
one, to get you out
of the uncanny valley

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.200
and, two, just make-- you
know, realize that it's Mark.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.980
And, kind of, some of
the first tests we did

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.350
and when we finally got all
of the software working,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.560
it just-- it's like magic.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.870
You know, you've
got him in the diner

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.480
and there's this one shot-- it's
my favorite shot in the film--

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.890
where he's, like--
you know, Hulk's there

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.590
eating a giant stack
of pancakes and eggs

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.010
and you've actually got the
digital character, you know,

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.720
talking with his
mouth full of food.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.780
You know, it's hard enough
to get the lip sync to work

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.070
correctly, but
suddenly he's actually,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.370
you know,
[MAKES GARBLED CHEWING NOISE]

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.070
around the food.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.480
And it's, like, oh,
my God, it's working.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.680
This is amazing.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.680
 I love that diner scene.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.760
As I was rewatching actually
where Mark is in the suit--

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.170
the motion capture suit--

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.460
I just got fascinated
with the fact

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.170
that there was an actual
crepe and then I--

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.030
you know, you watch
the final version,

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.450
and then there's this digital
crepe, and Hulk's hand.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.070
And I just find it fascinating
of how you and the team

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.610
put Hulk in this scene with
all these other characters

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.500
and how this all comes together.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.750
 The key to all this--
making all this work--

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:41.950
the biggest thing
you can do is make

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.480
the actor comfortable on set.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.490
They're in this giant helmet
with a camera on the front

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.950
of it and this crazy outfit
that we use to be able to track

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:48.980
it with cameras without--

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.980
you know, a lot of times people
will try to do that just in,

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.270
like, a motion capture volume
that's separate from set.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.720
And, if you can, you want
to be able to put them

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.910
on set with their
other actors, so they

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.980
can listen to each other
and play off each other.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.710
And then you give them
things like the crepes

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.030
and the sausages, so that they
can actually feel like they're

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.230
that character in scene.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.440
So, you know, it's a way to keep
the scene from becoming sterile

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.240
because a lot of times with
visual effects-- you know,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.840
when you just got a giant
blue screen out there,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:12.640
it's, kind of--

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.930
it'll take the actors out of it.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.150
 I want to talk
about the taco scene.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.010
One, the internet loves them--

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.830
that Hulk giving
tacos to Scott scene.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.150
I think it is wonderful.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.030
It's so sweet.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.620
And when you talk about,
you know, Mark taking Hulk

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.630
on this journey,
and the character,

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.010
and the way he's developed--

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.760
I also am just fascinated
by this massive CGI

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.140
character handing these
tiny little tacos.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.997
I assume that was all VFX?

00:03:38.997 --> 00:03:40.030
 Yeah, it was all VFX effects.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.660
We had, you know, Mark on set.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.100
We had real tacos that
he could hand to Paul.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.250
Sometimes you'll get lucky and
you can keep the real tacos,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.257
sometimes you can't.

00:03:48.257 --> 00:03:49.390
You know, with the
case with Hulk,

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:50.957
you know, his hand's so
much bigger than Mark--

00:03:50.957 --> 00:03:52.340
we ended up replacing
them and, you know,

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.920
it ended up being this
really sweet moment.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.510
 When we're talking about the
way that the Hulk has evolved--

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.470
now, we see our Hulk going
back to the Battle of New York

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:06.430
and seeing his more
aggressive side out.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.910
How do you, as the VFX
team, work with Mark

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.590
to actually express the--

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.053
the, sort of, reluctant Hulk.

00:04:16.053 --> 00:04:17.320
 If you look at the
different films--

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.600
you go from the first
Avengers, to Ultron,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.490
to Ragnarok-- you
know, the Hulk model

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.050
changed throughout the films.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.080
And so because we were
traveling back in time,

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.450
we actually just went
back and resurrected

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.210
the actual sculpt of Hulk
from the first Avengers

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.200
and started with that.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.760
And then when we
started with Smart Hulk,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.500
we knew he was going to be--

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.030
because of the merging
of Banner and Hulk,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.240
he'd be a little
bit smaller, right?

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.900
So the Hulk himself he's
about eight foot, 8 foot

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.550
5-- you know, he'll
slouch a little bit.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.030
If you compare him
to Thanos, he's

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.830
actually taller than Thanos
except when he's slouches.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.470
So he's a little
shorter than Thanos.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.970
So we made about 7 foot 5.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.690
So we used the proportions
from the first Avengers

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.150
and then, kind of, started
manipulating from there

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.870
and, kind of, putting some
more of Banner's humanity

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.680
into the Hulk-- into his sculpt.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.740
We got the first, kind of,
turntables back and on his face

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.547
you captured Mark,
you captured his face,

00:05:05.547 --> 00:05:07.380
and then when everybody saw
it, they all thought, well,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.510
he's really handsome, you know.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.370
And it was like-- and
we kept getting it,

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.567
so it's like-- you
know, Hulk's got game,

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.300
maybe he's going to go on
dates over the five years.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.900
 I imagine that you have
a computer that is just,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.840
like, Hulk and you have
sliders for, like, you know,

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.320
making him smaller, making them
larger, like, more handsome,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.240
more handsome, more handsome,
and he's, like, you know,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.830
hearts all around him and
everybody's, like, whoo--

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.010
I want that computer.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.360
I love all those
little details and I'm

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.490
sure we're going
to see tons more

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.530
when Marvel Studios'
Avengers Endgame hits

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.150
digital and Blu-ray.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.340
Thanks so much for
being here, Dan.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.460
- Thank you.
- Appreciate it.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

